WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.085 --> 00:00:07.546
Exactly 12 years ago in a TEDx Talk,

00:00:07.587 --> 00:00:10.465
I introduced my seven species of robot.

00:00:11.174 --> 00:00:13.468
A unique three-legged robot
called STriDER,

00:00:13.510 --> 00:00:15.637
a wheel-leg hybrid robot called IMPASS,

00:00:15.679 --> 00:00:17.556
an amoeba robot and many more.

00:00:18.098 --> 00:00:20.141
Now, 12 years is a pretty long time,

00:00:20.183 --> 00:00:22.811
especially in a cutting-edge
field like robotics.

00:00:22.852 --> 00:00:25.772
How have our robots evolved
during the past 12 years?

00:00:25.772 --> 00:00:28.692
What new species of robot
have we built since then?

00:00:29.067 --> 00:00:30.694
Hello, my name is Dennis Hong,

00:00:30.735 --> 00:00:34.990
and today, I would like to show you
our next seven species of robot.

00:00:36.241 --> 00:00:37.659
Since my last TEDx Talk,

00:00:37.701 --> 00:00:40.495
we’ve been focusing on the development
of humanoid robots:

00:00:40.495 --> 00:00:41.746
robots that look like us,

00:00:41.746 --> 00:00:43.873
two legs, torso, two arms and a head.

00:00:43.915 --> 00:00:45.917
Let me show you some
of the humanoid robots

00:00:45.959 --> 00:00:48.003
that we developed at our lab, RoMeLa.

00:00:48.628 --> 00:00:51.673
DARwIn is a miniature humanoid
robot for research and education

00:00:51.673 --> 00:00:53.800
that we made fully open-source.

00:00:53.842 --> 00:00:54.968
(Laughter)

00:00:54.968 --> 00:00:56.845
CHARLI is considered the United States’

00:00:56.845 --> 00:00:58.638
very first full-sized humanoid robot.

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:01.266
THOR-RD is for disaster
relief applications

00:01:01.308 --> 00:01:04.894
and Shipboard Autonomous Fire fighting
Robot, SAFFiR and THOR,

00:01:04.936 --> 00:01:07.564
which uses this unique
biologically-inspired actuator.

00:01:07.564 --> 00:01:08.732
And many more.

00:01:08.732 --> 00:01:12.068
I think we built more than a dozen
different types of humanoid  robots.

00:01:12.068 --> 00:01:16.197
However, after more than a decade
of humanoid robotics research,

00:01:16.239 --> 00:01:18.700
I'm starting to have different thoughts.

00:01:19.034 --> 00:01:21.494
Now humanoid robots
are important and they're great.

00:01:21.494 --> 00:01:24.831
However, they always fall down,
they’re too unstable.

00:01:24.873 --> 00:01:26.291
And they’re painfully slow.

00:01:26.333 --> 00:01:28.793
Yes, these days we’re trying
to develop technology

00:01:28.793 --> 00:01:30.420
that resolves these two problems,

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:33.882
but still, they are too expensive
and they’re too complicated.

00:01:33.882 --> 00:01:37.260
And most importantly,
they’re too dangerous.

00:01:37.302 --> 00:01:40.472
You do not want to be next to these big,
heavy moving robots.

00:01:40.513 --> 00:01:41.931
It might fall on top of you.

00:01:41.931 --> 00:01:43.224
So what do we do?

00:01:44.142 --> 00:01:46.144
(Laughter)

00:01:46.186 --> 00:01:50.315
Now, why is it so difficult to make
robots walk with two feet?

00:01:50.315 --> 00:01:52.067
I mean, we do it all the time.

00:01:52.067 --> 00:01:56.655
So once I wanted to throw away everything
I know about robotics for a moment

00:01:56.696 --> 00:01:58.490
and try to think fundamentally,

00:01:58.490 --> 00:02:01.201
why is it so difficult to make
robots walk with two feet?

00:02:01.201 --> 00:02:02.827
And I found something fascinating.

00:02:02.869 --> 00:02:04.245
So check this out.

00:02:04.287 --> 00:02:06.498
Now, when your robot
tries to move forward,

00:02:06.498 --> 00:02:08.750
one of the reasons why
it constantly falls down

00:02:08.792 --> 00:02:12.629
is because the distance
between your left and right leg.

00:02:12.629 --> 00:02:15.340
Because your legs move forward
and backward, up and down,

00:02:15.340 --> 00:02:17.342
it creates this unwanted twisting forces,

00:02:17.384 --> 00:02:19.344
we call these moments, right?

00:02:19.386 --> 00:02:22.430
Now, this is a video called
a RoboOne Competition.

00:02:22.472 --> 00:02:24.099
It's a robot fighting competition.

00:02:24.099 --> 00:02:27.268
And people who participate in this
competition are robot hobbyists.

00:02:27.310 --> 00:02:30.230
And if you look at the robots,
they develop, it’s brilliant.

00:02:30.230 --> 00:02:33.316
It's fast, it's nimble,
and it doesn't fall down.

00:02:33.358 --> 00:02:34.609
And if you look carefully,

00:02:34.651 --> 00:02:37.362
they always walk sideways,

00:02:37.362 --> 00:02:40.198
Because if you walk sideways,

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:44.703
your left and right leg line up,
so your twisting moments disappear.

00:02:45.036 --> 00:02:47.622
I actually got this inspiration
by watching sports,

00:02:47.664 --> 00:02:49.374
fencing or ballet.

00:02:49.416 --> 00:02:52.377
They always walk sideways,
and I think this is the reason why.

00:02:52.419 --> 00:02:55.922
Now, one of the problems of trying
to make robots move sideways

00:02:55.964 --> 00:02:57.966
is you cannot really use your knees.

00:02:58.007 --> 00:02:59.634
So what do we do?

00:02:59.634 --> 00:03:01.803
We rotate our legs.

00:03:01.803 --> 00:03:03.471
It looks kind of awkward, right?

00:03:03.513 --> 00:03:05.974
So actually, let's change
the entire torso like this

00:03:06.015 --> 00:03:07.350
and make it walk this way.

00:03:07.392 --> 00:03:09.561
Now, the problem
of this kind of configuration

00:03:09.602 --> 00:03:12.564
is that you really cannot use
your feet and ankles.

00:03:12.605 --> 00:03:13.898
So what do we do?

00:03:13.898 --> 00:03:15.275
We get rid of them.

00:03:15.275 --> 00:03:17.694
Now let's make more changes,
let's change the shin.

00:03:17.694 --> 00:03:19.779
And while doing that,
let's change the head.

00:03:19.779 --> 00:03:22.031
And something strange starts to emerge.

00:03:22.073 --> 00:03:25.785
This is NABi, Non Anthropomorphic Biped.

00:03:25.785 --> 00:03:27.829
In other words,
it's a robot with two legs,

00:03:27.871 --> 00:03:30.331
but it does not look like a human.

00:03:30.331 --> 00:03:31.583
How does it walk?

00:03:31.583 --> 00:03:32.751
It walks like this.

00:03:32.792 --> 00:03:35.170
Simple, fast, easy, low cost.

00:03:35.211 --> 00:03:36.337
This is great.

00:03:36.379 --> 00:03:38.882
Now, I briefly mentioned
about a robot called SAFFiR,

00:03:38.923 --> 00:03:41.509
Shipboard Autonomous
Fire Fighting Robot that we built.

00:03:41.509 --> 00:03:43.011
This is a robot for Navy ships.

00:03:43.052 --> 00:03:46.556
And I don't know if you've been
on a Navy ship, but if you open this door,

00:03:46.556 --> 00:03:48.141
it has a really high door seal.

00:03:48.141 --> 00:03:49.559
We call that “knee-knockers.”

00:03:49.559 --> 00:03:53.271
Before we had this robot, it was very
difficult for the robot to climb over it.

00:03:53.271 --> 00:03:56.274
But if a robot does not
have to be like a human,

00:03:56.274 --> 00:03:58.359
we can do something like this.

00:03:58.401 --> 00:04:00.695
Make the knee rotate at 360 degrees.

00:04:00.737 --> 00:04:01.863
Brilliant!

00:04:01.905 --> 00:04:03.072
Isn't this cool?

00:04:03.072 --> 00:04:04.073
(Laughs)

00:04:04.115 --> 00:04:05.283
Yeah.

00:04:05.325 --> 00:04:08.995
Our robots can climb stairs,
but it's rather difficult.

00:04:09.037 --> 00:04:11.581
But now we can climb stairs like this.

00:04:13.082 --> 00:04:15.710
Now, is this just simulation
and animation? No.

00:04:15.710 --> 00:04:19.547
In two weeks, from the idea,
analysis, design and fabrication,

00:04:19.547 --> 00:04:21.674
in two weeks, we built our first robot.

00:04:21.674 --> 00:04:24.010
And you have not seen anything like this.

00:04:31.643 --> 00:04:32.852
(Video) Man: You're wild.

00:04:32.894 --> 00:04:34.896
And we use many different types of tricks.

00:04:34.938 --> 00:04:37.190
For example, utilize
the springiness of the feet

00:04:37.190 --> 00:04:39.818
we can store the energy
and make it hop and jump.

00:04:44.280 --> 00:04:46.741
When we first introduced
NABi to the community,

00:04:46.783 --> 00:04:48.952
it had a pretty good impact
on the community.

00:04:48.993 --> 00:04:52.914
However, this robot is great
for walking forward and backward.

00:04:52.956 --> 00:04:54.791
But how does it change directions?

00:04:54.791 --> 00:04:55.959
It can't.

00:04:56.376 --> 00:05:01.047
So we decided to work on the next
version called ALPHRED.

00:05:01.047 --> 00:05:04.592
ALPHRED stands for Autonomous
Leg Personal Helper Robot

00:05:04.592 --> 00:05:05.802
with Enhanced Dynamics.

00:05:05.802 --> 00:05:09.764
So think of this robot as two NABis
stacked onto each other.

00:05:09.764 --> 00:05:10.974
So it looks like this.

00:05:11.015 --> 00:05:12.851
So this robot is axisymmetric,

00:05:12.892 --> 00:05:15.728
which means that there's no forwards,
backwards or sideways.

00:05:15.770 --> 00:05:18.314
Now, when it walks,
it has a very wide stance, right?

00:05:18.356 --> 00:05:19.566
So it can walk like this.

00:05:19.607 --> 00:05:22.360
And this is great for unstructured
outdoor environments.

00:05:22.402 --> 00:05:23.778
However, it's pretty slow.

00:05:24.153 --> 00:05:27.824
If you want to make it go really fast,
you can change your body configuration.

00:05:27.866 --> 00:05:29.617
And if you go like this,

00:05:29.617 --> 00:05:32.620
you can have your forward legs
and backward legs,

00:05:32.620 --> 00:05:34.789
and then you can gallop like a horse.

00:05:41.421 --> 00:05:43.089
There you go. (Laughs)

00:05:43.131 --> 00:05:45.842
And when you lift two of the arms
it can walk like NABi,

00:05:45.884 --> 00:05:48.761
then the two of the other legs
can be used as arms

00:05:48.803 --> 00:05:50.680
to pick up boxes or press buttons.

00:05:50.722 --> 00:05:53.641
And we're pretty excited about this
new type of configuration.

00:05:53.641 --> 00:05:56.811
We call this multimodal locomotion,
and this is ALPHRED.

00:05:56.811 --> 00:05:58.521
Now, while developing these robots,

00:05:58.563 --> 00:06:01.065
we've been also working
on this new type of actuator.

00:06:01.107 --> 00:06:03.985
Now, when we say actuators,
we're talking about these devices

00:06:04.027 --> 00:06:06.696
that make the robots move
just like muscles in humans.

00:06:06.696 --> 00:06:08.865
Now, most of the robots that exist today,

00:06:08.907 --> 00:06:11.868
electric robots, use electric motors
with gears, right?

00:06:11.868 --> 00:06:13.369
And these are really strong,

00:06:13.369 --> 00:06:15.955
and they're precise,
they're good for manufacturing.

00:06:15.997 --> 00:06:18.041
But for legs, we need something different.

00:06:18.082 --> 00:06:22.462
We need something like biological muscles
that's compliant, springy,

00:06:22.503 --> 00:06:25.048
and not only the position,
but also change the force.

00:06:25.089 --> 00:06:28.843
And we developed a new type of actuator
called the BEAR actuator.

00:06:29.302 --> 00:06:32.513
Now, using this BEAR
actuator with the NABi,

00:06:32.555 --> 00:06:35.266
we developed a second version
called NABi-2.

00:06:35.808 --> 00:06:38.144
Now, if you remember,
NABi was our experiment

00:06:38.186 --> 00:06:41.230
for studying a new type
of bipedal locomotion.

00:06:41.272 --> 00:06:44.567
But it turns out that this NABi
with these new actuators

00:06:44.567 --> 00:06:47.153
doesn't work very well
in terms of walking.

00:06:47.153 --> 00:06:49.781
However, unexpectedly,
we didn't know this,

00:06:49.822 --> 00:06:53.242
but this robot is great
for jumping and hopping.

00:06:53.284 --> 00:06:56.412
It can jump forward, backward
and turn directions, it's brilliant.

00:06:56.412 --> 00:06:59.332
But the really cool thing is,
once it jumps and when it lands,

00:06:59.332 --> 00:07:03.586
the motors become generators,
and the impact is changed to electricity.

00:07:03.628 --> 00:07:06.506
And it can charge its battery,
so it’s very energy efficient.

00:07:09.050 --> 00:07:11.344
And NABi-2 is ready to go.

00:07:22.563 --> 00:07:24.816
Or not. (Laughs)

00:07:24.857 --> 00:07:26.317
But this is NABi-2.

00:07:26.359 --> 00:07:29.112
So BEAR actuator with NABi was NABi-2,

00:07:29.153 --> 00:07:32.240
then BEAR actuator with ALPHRED is --

00:07:32.281 --> 00:07:33.449
ALPHRED-2.

00:07:33.491 --> 00:07:35.493
And this is the next
generation of ALPHRED.

00:07:35.535 --> 00:07:38.830
So this is the very first time
we turned on the switch.

00:07:38.871 --> 00:07:41.582
Look carefully at the reaction
of our students.

00:07:46.838 --> 00:07:49.048
(Video) Man: Whoa! Oh, my God!

00:07:49.090 --> 00:07:50.216
What the -- ?

00:07:50.258 --> 00:07:51.968
Dennis Hong: Excuse me. (Laughs)

00:07:52.010 --> 00:07:54.012
(Video) (Man shouts excitedly)

00:07:54.679 --> 00:07:55.847
Oh, my!

00:07:55.847 --> 00:07:57.015
Man 2: That was amazing!

00:07:57.056 --> 00:07:58.766
I think it jumped about 1.4 meters.

00:07:58.766 --> 00:07:59.892
This is remarkable.

00:07:59.934 --> 00:08:02.353
Walking is also very fast and stable.

00:08:05.648 --> 00:08:07.817
So we wanted to do something
more interesting.

00:08:07.859 --> 00:08:09.485
We wanted to make it do taekwondo.

00:08:12.864 --> 00:08:15.825
(Video) (Men shouting excitedly) Whoa!

00:08:17.535 --> 00:08:19.829
DH: We're just having too much fun.

00:08:19.871 --> 00:08:21.831
And ALPHRED-2 is ready to go.

00:08:40.141 --> 00:08:42.477
Now, ALPHRED-2 is actually
a very practical robot.

00:08:42.518 --> 00:08:45.146
For example, this is an example
of picking up a box,

00:08:45.188 --> 00:08:48.316
delivering it and putting it nicely
on the ground or on the table.

00:08:48.316 --> 00:08:50.526
I sped up the video
because it was pretty slow,

00:08:50.526 --> 00:08:53.488
but you can get an idea how we can use
these new type of robots

00:08:53.488 --> 00:08:54.906
for different applications.

00:08:54.947 --> 00:08:56.699
And this is ALPHRED-2.

00:08:56.699 --> 00:08:58.409
Now, we looked at two legs.

00:08:58.451 --> 00:09:00.119
We looked at four legs.

00:09:00.161 --> 00:09:01.913
What about six legs?

00:09:01.954 --> 00:09:03.998
Of course, we have a hexapod robot.

00:09:04.040 --> 00:09:07.710
Now, this robot, HEX,
is about the size of a small car.

00:09:07.752 --> 00:09:10.922
Now, the cool thing
about six-legged robots is this.

00:09:10.963 --> 00:09:13.174
Now, to have static stability,

00:09:13.216 --> 00:09:15.885
the minimum number of contacts
with the ground is three.

00:09:15.927 --> 00:09:18.096
Think of a camera tripod.

00:09:18.137 --> 00:09:19.639
If you have a hexapod robot,

00:09:19.639 --> 00:09:22.350
you can always have three-point
contact with the ground.

00:09:22.391 --> 00:09:23.518
Three, three, three.

00:09:23.559 --> 00:09:26.145
And this makes it really great
for outdoor environment,

00:09:26.145 --> 00:09:27.313
rough environment.

00:09:27.355 --> 00:09:30.525
This particular robot we're developing
for demining applications,

00:09:30.566 --> 00:09:33.778
getting rid of landmines
underground autonomously.

00:09:38.282 --> 00:09:40.368
If you see it in person,
it’s kind of scary,

00:09:40.368 --> 00:09:41.744
it's big and goes like this.

00:09:41.744 --> 00:09:42.829
(Laughs)

00:09:42.829 --> 00:09:43.996
So this is HEX.

00:09:44.038 --> 00:09:45.540
We also have a smaller version,

00:09:45.540 --> 00:09:49.168
this is called SiLVIA: Six Legged
Vehicle with Intelligent Articulation.

00:09:49.210 --> 00:09:51.921
And as I mentioned, these type
of robots are really great

00:09:51.921 --> 00:09:54.465
for unstructured terrain such as this.

00:09:56.384 --> 00:09:58.636
It can also climb up and down stairs.

00:10:00.888 --> 00:10:05.434
And this robot might be the strongest
six-legged robot out there.

00:10:05.476 --> 00:10:07.478
(Video) Man: Ah, 20 kilograms.

00:10:11.357 --> 00:10:14.068
DH: Now, the reason why
we wanted to make it really strong

00:10:14.110 --> 00:10:18.281
is because we want this robot to do some
very interesting new type of stuff.

00:10:20.658 --> 00:10:24.912
So this is the very first robot that can
brace between walls and climb up.

00:10:24.912 --> 00:10:27.165
So it's like Spiderman, isn't that cool?

00:10:28.624 --> 00:10:30.293
And it has some other tricks, too.

00:10:30.334 --> 00:10:33.838
If you put suction cups on the feet,
it can stick to the wall and climb.

00:10:34.297 --> 00:10:35.548
And if you give it an arm,

00:10:35.590 --> 00:10:38.885
it can do some fun stuff
on the blackboard, too.

00:10:39.385 --> 00:10:40.595
And this is SiLVIA.

00:10:40.636 --> 00:10:42.930
So we talked about vertical mobility.

00:10:42.972 --> 00:10:46.767
Twelve years ago, in my last TEDx Talk,
I introduced a robot called CLIMBeR.

00:10:46.809 --> 00:10:50.730
It's a robot for climbing cliffs
using cables like a human.

00:10:50.771 --> 00:10:53.649
But this year, we wanted
to get rid of the cable.

00:10:53.691 --> 00:10:56.027
We wanted to make it do free climbing.

00:10:56.068 --> 00:10:59.322
And I'm very excited to introduce
a brand new robot called SCALER,

00:10:59.322 --> 00:11:02.450
Spine Enhanced Climbing Autonomous
Legged Exploration Robot.

00:11:02.491 --> 00:11:03.993
Now, when you first look at it,

00:11:04.035 --> 00:11:06.746
it looks like a quadruped robot,
four-legged robot.

00:11:06.787 --> 00:11:09.540
It's actually very strong,
but strength is not all.

00:11:09.582 --> 00:11:11.626
We had to develop
this new type of gripper,

00:11:11.667 --> 00:11:13.085
we call these spine grippers,

00:11:13.085 --> 00:11:15.171
that can really grab onto the surfaces.

00:11:16.255 --> 00:11:17.423
And this video,

00:11:17.465 --> 00:11:19.133
we just took it a few days ago.

00:11:19.175 --> 00:11:22.261
So literally, it's taking
its very first few steps.

00:11:22.303 --> 00:11:25.431
Now, why are we interested
in cliff-climbing robots?

00:11:25.431 --> 00:11:26.807
Rescue missions, of course.

00:11:26.849 --> 00:11:30.019
But we're really interested
in the planetary applications.

00:11:30.061 --> 00:11:31.312
Now, as you know, on Mars,

00:11:31.354 --> 00:11:33.981
we had these Rovers
discovering the new planet.

00:11:34.023 --> 00:11:38.152
However, the really science-rich sites
are always at the cliffs,

00:11:38.194 --> 00:11:39.820
and these Rovers cannot get there.

00:11:39.820 --> 00:11:42.698
So something like this will enable
those kind of exploration,

00:11:42.740 --> 00:11:45.368
in the science-rich sites
on planetary surfaces.

00:11:45.409 --> 00:11:46.535
This is SCALER.

00:11:46.577 --> 00:11:50.248
Now, we talked about legs,
but what about wheels?

00:11:50.289 --> 00:11:52.124
Of course, we have robots with wheels.

00:11:52.124 --> 00:11:53.626
This is called OmBURo,

00:11:53.668 --> 00:11:57.088
Omnidirectional Balancing Unicycle Robot.

00:11:57.088 --> 00:11:59.966
And as the name implies, it has one wheel,

00:11:59.966 --> 00:12:01.259
it's omnidirectional,

00:12:01.300 --> 00:12:03.761
it can go forward, backwards,
sideways and diagonal.

00:12:03.803 --> 00:12:07.640
But if we look at it, it doesn’t even
need to change its orientation.

00:12:07.640 --> 00:12:09.558
When you see it, it's almost like magic.

00:12:09.600 --> 00:12:11.811
It feels like it's gliding
over the surface.

00:12:11.811 --> 00:12:12.979
Isn't that cool?

00:12:12.979 --> 00:12:15.773
It can also balance
and climb very steep surfaces.

00:12:16.941 --> 00:12:20.027
Now it follows path,
now, yes, it has one wheel,

00:12:20.069 --> 00:12:22.571
but actually has these smaller
rollers in the rim

00:12:22.613 --> 00:12:24.573
to generate this kind of motion as well.

00:12:24.615 --> 00:12:27.118
So why do we want to build
a robot like this?

00:12:27.451 --> 00:12:30.663
We like to have robots living with us
in this environment,

00:12:30.705 --> 00:12:34.125
and this environment sometimes is crowded,
a lot of people or obstacles.

00:12:34.166 --> 00:12:37.962
To go through people
or to avoid obstacles,

00:12:38.004 --> 00:12:40.840
it's very important to have
a very small cross section,

00:12:40.840 --> 00:12:44.677
and probably a single wheel is the best
way to have a very small cross section.

00:12:44.719 --> 00:12:46.429
And this is OmBURo.

00:12:46.429 --> 00:12:49.640
Now, I’ve shown you many different shapes,
sizes, mechanism robots.

00:12:49.640 --> 00:12:51.976
And if you remember this,
the whole thing started

00:12:51.976 --> 00:12:55.146
because we had problems
with this bipedal locomotion.

00:12:55.146 --> 00:13:00.192
Is there a way to make
these humanoid robots not fall at all?

00:13:00.234 --> 00:13:01.402
There is.

00:13:01.444 --> 00:13:04.363
And this robot is called BALLU.

00:13:09.493 --> 00:13:11.120
This is my son, Ethan, by the way.

00:13:11.162 --> 00:13:13.622
(Video) Ethan (Clapping and cheering)

00:13:14.832 --> 00:13:15.958
Man: Uh-oh.

00:13:16.709 --> 00:13:18.878
DH: What if robots never fell?

00:13:20.338 --> 00:13:23.507
What if we could change
the direction of gravity?

00:13:23.549 --> 00:13:27.094
I'm very excited to show you
our new robot, BALLU,

00:13:27.094 --> 00:13:29.138
Buoyancy Assisted Lightweight Leg Unit.

00:13:29.472 --> 00:13:32.558
This robot is a bipedal robot
the size of a human being.

00:13:32.600 --> 00:13:35.061
But the body is a helium balloon.

00:13:35.102 --> 00:13:37.021
It's a balloon with two legs.

00:13:37.855 --> 00:13:39.023
How does it walk?

00:13:39.023 --> 00:13:40.775
It walks like this.

00:13:40.816 --> 00:13:42.485
Very elegant, isn't it?

00:13:43.444 --> 00:13:46.072
This robot might be the safest
robot out there.

00:13:46.072 --> 00:13:48.032
It can walk forward, backwards, sideways.

00:13:48.074 --> 00:13:49.450
It can turn directions.

00:13:53.579 --> 00:13:55.623
We want this robot to be living with us.

00:13:55.664 --> 00:13:58.125
So it can climb upstairs, downstairs.

00:13:58.501 --> 00:14:00.002
Go over obstacles.

00:14:04.924 --> 00:14:06.717
It's fun to play with.

00:14:11.722 --> 00:14:14.600
It can hop, now it can hop up
to a height of a table top.

00:14:15.684 --> 00:14:17.395
Oh, this robot is a good dancer.

00:14:17.395 --> 00:14:21.273
If you turn on the music, it listens
to beat and it dances to the beat.

00:14:21.273 --> 00:14:23.067
(Music)

00:14:23.109 --> 00:14:26.070
Now we do crazy things
when we do experiments.

00:14:26.112 --> 00:14:29.698
Once, we opened the window
and threw the robot out the window.

00:14:35.830 --> 00:14:36.997
Isn't it great?

00:14:37.039 --> 00:14:39.458
It solves all the problems
of bilateral locomotion.

00:14:39.458 --> 00:14:41.043
It’s cheap, it doesn’t fall down.

00:14:41.043 --> 00:14:43.754
However, it also creates new problems.

00:14:43.796 --> 00:14:45.798
This robot is horrible outdoors

00:14:45.840 --> 00:14:48.968
because if the wind blows, it floats away.

00:14:53.013 --> 00:14:55.808
Now, these are some of the images
of our secret projects.

00:14:55.850 --> 00:14:57.226
I'm not going to explain it,

00:14:57.226 --> 00:15:00.896
but we’re using projection mapping,
vortex cannons, many different things.

00:15:00.938 --> 00:15:04.984
Oh, this robot literally
can walk on water.

00:15:04.984 --> 00:15:07.736
You put ping-pong balls on the foot,
it can walk on water.

00:15:07.778 --> 00:15:09.780
It can also walk on a tightrope.

00:15:10.698 --> 00:15:11.866
Isn’t this cool?

00:15:11.866 --> 00:15:13.033
This is BALLU.

00:15:13.617 --> 00:15:17.288
By the way, all of our videos
end with a bang like this.

00:15:20.583 --> 00:15:22.251
Isn’t that cool? This is BALLU.

00:15:22.251 --> 00:15:23.419
(Laughs)

00:15:24.211 --> 00:15:25.379
Thank you.

00:15:25.379 --> 00:15:29.467
(Applause)

00:15:29.508 --> 00:15:32.261
Now all of these new
inventions and creativity

00:15:32.303 --> 00:15:34.388
came from the problem of humanoid robots.

00:15:34.388 --> 00:15:39.059
However, secretly, we've still been
working on humanoid robots.

00:15:39.059 --> 00:15:41.854
And this robot is called
ARTEMIS, our latest one,

00:15:41.854 --> 00:15:44.190
Advanced Robotics Technology
for Enhanced Mobility

00:15:44.231 --> 00:15:45.399
and Improved Stability.

00:15:45.399 --> 00:15:48.652
And this is the very first time
I'm showing it to the general public.

00:15:48.694 --> 00:15:50.863
This is a sneak peek
of what is about to come.

00:15:50.863 --> 00:15:53.616
Now, this robot, ARTEMIS,
uses a new type of technology.

00:15:53.616 --> 00:15:55.618
It's based on that BEAR actuator.

00:15:55.618 --> 00:15:58.704
And because it's so new, you cannot
buy any of these components.

00:15:58.746 --> 00:16:02.500
So we have to design and fabricate
every single part of this robot.

00:16:05.711 --> 00:16:09.298
We have just finished assembly
of the lower body,

00:16:09.340 --> 00:16:14.762
and I'm very excited to show you
a sneak peek of robot ARTEMIS.

00:16:14.803 --> 00:16:17.723
(Suspenseful music)

00:16:36.158 --> 00:16:39.411
And ARTEMIS is coming too,
I cannot wait to show it to you,

00:16:39.453 --> 00:16:41.413
probably on stage in my next talk.

00:16:41.872 --> 00:16:45.876
I've shown you a lot of our newest
creation robots from our lab RoMeLa.

00:16:45.918 --> 00:16:48.170
I would like to give credit
to our brilliant,

00:16:48.212 --> 00:16:49.880
hardworking students at RoMeLa.

00:16:49.922 --> 00:16:53.717
But what is the source of this creativity?

00:16:53.759 --> 00:16:55.344
What's the source of this energy?

00:16:55.386 --> 00:16:58.264
What is the secret
to this crazy productivity?

00:16:58.722 --> 00:17:02.017
I think the next video
will be able to answer that question.

00:17:02.977 --> 00:17:04.520
(Music)

00:17:05.312 --> 00:17:06.564
(Video) Man: Look at that.

00:17:06.605 --> 00:17:07.773
DH: You remember BALLU?

00:17:07.773 --> 00:17:09.608
We're doing the experiment of dancing.

00:17:09.650 --> 00:17:12.236
By the way, this is Saturday,
2am in the morning.

00:17:12.278 --> 00:17:16.574
(Music)

00:17:16.991 --> 00:17:18.242
(Laughter)

00:17:18.284 --> 00:17:19.577
We're having too much fun,

00:17:19.577 --> 00:17:22.288
so, yeah, let's bring out
all of our robots, one by one.

00:17:22.288 --> 00:17:25.916
(Music)

00:17:26.542 --> 00:17:28.002
You remember NABi?

00:17:28.043 --> 00:17:30.588
(Music)

00:17:40.139 --> 00:17:42.099
An early prototype of ALPHRED.

00:17:42.099 --> 00:17:43.559
(Music continues)

00:17:46.228 --> 00:17:48.314
Everybody's favorite, DARwIn-OP.

00:17:52.943 --> 00:17:55.029
The rotating knee mechanism for NABi.

00:17:55.446 --> 00:17:57.072
(Music continues)

00:18:00.451 --> 00:18:02.161
The six-legged hexapod robot.

00:18:02.661 --> 00:18:04.038
(Music continues)

00:18:08.167 --> 00:18:11.629
This is RoMeLa, the Robotics
and Mechanism Laboratory at UCLA.

00:18:12.004 --> 00:18:13.714
(Music continues)

00:18:16.258 --> 00:18:19.345
By the way, that's me
on the left, dancing.

00:18:19.386 --> 00:18:22.848
You probably haven't seen a professor
dance and party with their students

00:18:22.848 --> 00:18:25.684
while conducting serious
experiments, right?

00:18:26.685 --> 00:18:30.272
Openness, freedom, trust, having fun

00:18:30.314 --> 00:18:33.776
and truly believing what you do
can change the world.

00:18:33.817 --> 00:18:38.656
These are some of the secrets
behind our next seven species of robot.

00:18:38.697 --> 00:18:39.865
Thank you very much.

00:18:39.907 --> 00:18:41.033
(Applause)

